For anyone looking for a solution to block non-SSO registrations after upgrading to V2, here is my temporary workaround.

### Managing Existing Users
If you want to delete unauthorized users who have already registered, the most direct way is through database operations (**please proceed with caution and back up your data first**):

1.  **Step 1: Confirm User ID**
    `SELECT id, full_name, email FROM users WHERE email = 'target@example.com';`
    *(Note: Verify carefully to avoid deleting the wrong user with similar names).*
2.  **Step 2: Execute Deletion**
    `DELETE FROM accounts WHERE user_id = 'YOUR_USER_ID';`
    `DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 'YOUR_USER_ID';`

---

### Blocking New Registrations via Nginx
If your LobeChat instance is deployed behind a reverse proxy (such as Nginx), you can block the built-in registration feature by adding the following configuration blocks.

#### Option 1: Basic Block (403 Forbidden)
This method directly rejects any access to the signup page and its API.

```nginx
# 1. Block the frontend registration page
location = /signup {
    return 403;
}

# 2. Block all POST requests starting with sign-up (API level)
location ~* ^/api/auth/sign-up {
    if ($request_method = POST) {
        return 403;
    }
}
```

#### Option 2: User-Friendly Block (Custom Error Page)
If you want the error to be more readable rather than just showing a generic 403 page:

```nginx
# Define a dedicated location for "Registration Disabled" notice
location @registration_disabled {
    default_type text/html;
    return 200 "
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
    <head>
        <meta charset='UTF-8'>
        <title>Registration Disabled</title>
        <style>
            body { font-family: -apple-system, sans-serif; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; height: 100vh; margin: 0; background: #f5f5f5; }
            .card { background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 12px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); text-align: center; max-width: 400px; }
            h1 { color: #333; font-size: 1.5rem; }
            p { color: #666; margin: 1.5rem 0; line-height: 1.6; }
            a { display: inline-block; background: #1677ff; color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; }
            a:hover { background: #4096ff; }
        </style>
    </head>
    <body>
        <div class='card'>
            <h1>Registration Disabled</h1>
            <p>This instance only supports <strong>SSO (Single Sign-On)</strong>. Direct registration is not accepted.</p>
            <a href='/signin'>Back to Login</a>
        </div>
    </body>
    </html>";
}

# --- Intercept Paths ---

# 1. Intercept the registration UI
location = /signup {
    error_page 403 = @registration_disabled;
    return 403;
}

# 2. Intercept the registration API endpoint
location ~* ^/api/auth/sign-up {
    if ($request_method = POST) {
        add_header 'Content-Type' 'application/json; charset=UTF-8';
        return 403 '{"error": "Signup is disabled. Please use SSO."}';
    }
}
```

**Note:** This is only a temporary workaround. There might be bypass methods, and it could stop working with future official updates.

---

**Personal Thoughts:**
I still hope the official team considers adding a native option to disable registration, rather than using a "high-friction" workaround to push a basic security/management feature behind a paywall (especially since this functionality was effectively available in V1). I have noticed many users complaining about this specific change recently. #11789 #11846 #11860  #11979 #11972

